# Verify Before Claiming

A passing `tsc`/build is NOT evidence of correctness. It only proves types compile.
Never say "done", "correct", "fixed", or "this works" based on a build alone.

## When refactoring or moving code

- BEFORE editing, capture the original behavior. Run `git diff` / `git show` on the
  exact lines you are changing and read them.
- AFTER editing, run `git diff HEAD -- <file>` and read every removed line. For each
  removed line ask: "Was this load-bearing? What relied on it?"
- If you delete or relocate a call (e.g. an `init()`, a side effect, an event
  listener), prove the behavior still happens on every path it happened before.

## When making a factual claim about the code

- Trace the actual runtime control flow, including the empty/first-run/no-permission
  path. Do not reason from intent or memory.
- If you claim "X was already like this" or "I didn't change X", you MUST verify with
  `git diff`/`git log` first. No claims about history without checking it.

## Watch for dependency cycles

A guard must not gate the very code that satisfies the guard. Example of the bug this
rule exists to prevent:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: checkPermissions() returns false until init() runs setup(),
// but init() is gated behind checkPermissions() -> init() never runs.
if (await this.nativePhone.checkPermissions()) {
  await this.nativePhone.init(); // init() is what registers the account
}

// ✅ GOOD: run the setup that registers the account; gate only user prompts.
await this.nativePhone.init();
```

## State limits honestly

When reporting results, separate what was verified from what was not:
- "Build passes" ≠ "verified at runtime".
- Call out untested paths and assumptions explicitly instead of implying completeness.
